    "innovation": "This paper uses a nine-member GCM ensemble to disentangle the roles of remote sea surface temperature forcing versus internal atmospheric variability in modulating spring precipitation in the La Plata Basin, showing how selecting observation-consistent members improves the analysis by reducing climate noise.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SINTEXG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "ECHAM4",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "The twentieth-century coupled model simulation (hereafter SSXX) has been performed with the coupled atmosphere-ocean general circulation model SINTEXG (Gualdi et al. 2008). It includes prescribed concentration of greenhouse gases (i.e., CO2, CH4, N2O and chlorofluorocarbon) and sulfate aerosols, as specified for the 20C3M experiment defined for the IPCC AR4 simulations (see http://www-pcmdi.llnl.gov/ipcc/about_ipcc.php for more details). The characteristics of both atmospheric and oceanic model components are described in previous publications (Cherchi et al. 2008; Gualdi et al. 2008). In particular, the atmospheric component is the same used for the AMIP-type simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SINTEXG",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "OPA",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "The twentieth-century coupled model simulation (hereafter SSXX) has been performed with the coupled atmosphere-ocean general circulation model SINTEXG (Gualdi et al. 2008). It includes prescribed concentration of greenhouse gases (i.e., CO2, CH4, N2O and chlorofluorocarbon) and sulfate aerosols, as specified for the 20C3M experiment defined for the IPCC AR4 simulations (see http://www-pcmdi.llnl.gov/ipcc/about_ipcc.php for more details). The characteristics of both atmospheric and oceanic model components are described in previous publications (Cherchi et al. 2008; Gualdi et al. 2008). In particular, the atmospheric component is the same used for the AMIP-type simulations. The oceanic component is OPA (Madec et al. 1998), which is spatially distributed over a three-dimensional Arakawa-C-type grid (about 2\u00b0 \u00d7 2\u00b0 horizontal resolution, with a meridional refinement of 0.5\u00b0 at the equator) and 31 prescribed vertical levels.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
